{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgdPJt938smzteiXRjqCw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 05 is done and the gate passed. Six items remain, so no completion marker.\n\n**What was done.** `lean/MOSPFormalization/DegreeBound/Potential.lean` defines the potential `f`, the per-component potential `f⁻` exactly as the plan states it, and proves 54 theorems with no `sorry` and no `axiom`, every one on the standard three axioms. They cover the one-deletion accounting (P1, P2, and P2r as an equality needing only the neighbours of the deleted vertex), the per-component and per-vertex forms of `f⁻`, its comparison with the global variant `F`, additivity over disjoint supports (`potentialMinus_add`, the statement the item flagged as the one that had to fit), and the cubic value and the `5/6` floor.\n\n**Checked before stated.** Every statement was first checked in exact rationals on 20,000 random graphs with isolated vertices at up to 12 vertices, 10,000 disjoint unions and 1,996 embedded cubic graphs, with zero violations. The one statement beyond item 02's checks is the neighbours-only P2r, re-checked in that form. The sandwich stage pins the transcribed Lean definition to the Python `fminus` on 500 graphs. The gate passed: 1,787 MOSP tests, 123 pathwidth-solver tests, 425 theorems in the axiom audit, paper untouched.\n\n**Two design notes for later items,** recorded in PROGRESS.md and report §5.4. Additivity is proved through a per-vertex spreading of each component's term, which avoids any bijection between component types. No `restrict` is defined. Item 08's disconnected case can define it and use `potentialMinus_add`, since the two halves have disjoint supports by construction.\n\n**Next** is item 06, the series-parallel reduction system."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":871,"cache_read_input